# Shell scripts must keep LF endings in every working tree.
#
# The blobs are already LF, but core.autocrlf=true rewrites them to CRLF on a
# Windows checkout, and a CRLF shebang is not executable on Linux: the kernel
# reads the interpreter as "bash\r" and execve fails with
#   /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
# which surfaces as exit 127. The target runtime for these installers is
# Dell/Ubuntu, and they are also run from WSL against the Windows tree, so the
# ending has to be pinned rather than left to the checkout platform.
#
# Deliberately scoped to shell scripts. A repository-wide `* text=auto` would
# renormalise 127 of the 445 tracked Python files, conflicting with in-flight
# branches and rewriting blame; that remains a separate decision.
*.sh text eol=lf
